# Custom Suricata rules, tasks 1.12 and 1.15.
# Written by Claude at Marko's explicit request.
# Only traffic the firewall allowed reaches these rules. TLS ends at Nginx,
# so they see TLS metadata and behaviour, not web payloads.
#
# Re-tested against real Nginx on 2026-11-04 (decisions 79 to 82). Nginx
# answers any non-TLS bytes on 443 with an HTTP 400, and Suricata's protocol
# detection looks at both directions, so the server's reply can label a
# garbage stream "http".

# --- Non-TLS on 443 (decision 79) ---------------------------------------------
# Two rules, one alert per flow. Both test and set mb.nontls, and Suricata
# runs rules that set a flowbit before rules that check it, so whichever
# matches first is the only one that fires on that flow.

# A stream that neither side makes recognisable, such as a tunnel whose
# server doesn't answer like Nginx. With Nginx in front, binary garbage
# usually ends up as 1000005 instead, because Nginx's HTTP 400 labels the
# flow before detection fails on the client's side (live and in
# binary-nginx400.pcap). Alert only: it depends on detection failing, which
# is lower confidence than a positive match. "failed" waits for detection to finish;
# "!tls" also fired on normal TLS, on packets sent before detection finished.
alert tcp 10.8.0.0/23 any -> 10.0.2.10 443 (msg:"MAILBRIDGE unidentified protocol on proxy 443"; flow:established,to_server; app-layer-protocol:failed; flowbits:isnotset,mb.nontls; flowbits:set,mb.nontls; classtype:policy-violation; sid:1000001; rev:2;)

# Plain HTTP on 443, or any garbage, text or binary, that Nginx's HTTP 400
# reply made Suricata label http (seen live with "hello" and random bytes). Nothing
# legitimate does this, and Nginx refuses it anyway, so it drops one hop
# earlier (decision 81).
drop http 10.8.0.0/23 any -> 10.0.2.10 443 (msg:"MAILBRIDGE non-TLS or plain HTTP on proxy 443"; flow:established,to_server; flowbits:isnotset,mb.nontls; flowbits:set,mb.nontls; classtype:policy-violation; sid:1000005; rev:2;)

# --- TLS ClientHello ---------------------------------------------------------

# TLS 1.0 client. A current browser doesn't offer this, so it suggests a
# scanner or a downgrade attempt. Nginx accepts only TLS 1.3 and refuses it.
# ssl_state:client_hello (rev 3): without it, a ClientHello split over two
# TCP segments matched on the first one, where only the record header is
# known, and every client's record header says 0x0301 ("1.0"). As a drop
# (rev 2) that blocked normal TLS 1.3 clients with large post-quantum key
# shares and took Mailbridge down on 2026-11-04 (decision 82). Alert only.
alert tls 10.8.0.0/23 any -> 10.0.2.10 443 (msg:"MAILBRIDGE TLS 1.0 client to proxy"; flow:established,to_server; ssl_state:client_hello; tls.version:1.0; classtype:policy-violation; sid:1000002; rev:3;)

# Server name other than proxy's two sites, mail.<domain> and vuln.<domain>
# (rev 2, task 2.11: vuln is a real site, so every visit alerted). Each
# negated content is anchored at the start of the name, so both must fail to
# match: the name starts with neither "mail." nor "vuln.", in any case
# (nocase: DNS names and Nginx's server_name ignore case). Only the prefix is
# matched, so the real domain stays out of this public repo; the cost is that
# mail.evil.example passes too, and Nginx then refuses that handshake. A
# ClientHello without SNI has no tls.sni buffer, so this rule can't match it
# (the no-SNI gap; 1000006 is disabled, decision 82). Alert only.
alert tls 10.8.0.0/23 any -> 10.0.2.10 443 (msg:"MAILBRIDGE unexpected TLS server name"; flow:established,to_server; tls.sni; content:!"mail."; nocase; startswith; content:!"vuln."; nocase; startswith; classtype:policy-violation; sid:1000003; rev:2;)

# ClientHello without SNI (decision 80): DISABLED on 2026-11-04 (decision 82).
# It fired on a normal ClientHello split over two TCP segments, apparently
# because ssl_state:client_hello is true before the helper has seen the SNI.
# Not proven, and no fix is tested yet, so the no-SNI gap is open again
# (TASKS open item). It returns only once tls13-big-2seg.pcap stays silent.
# Design kept for the rewrite: a silent helper (1000007) marks flows that send
# a name, and 1000006 alerts on a ClientHello from an unmarked flow.
# alert tls 10.8.0.0/23 any -> 10.0.2.10 443 (msg:"MAILBRIDGE helper: TLS server name present"; flow:established,to_server; tls.sni; content:"."; flowbits:set,mb.sni; flowbits:noalert; classtype:policy-violation; sid:1000007; rev:1;)
# alert tls 10.8.0.0/23 any -> 10.0.2.10 443 (msg:"MAILBRIDGE TLS client without server name"; flow:established,to_server; ssl_state:client_hello; flowbits:isnotset,mb.sni; flowbits:isnotset,mb.nosni; flowbits:set,mb.nosni; classtype:policy-violation; sid:1000006; rev:1;)

# --- Volume ------------------------------------------------------------------

# Connection flood from one client. A first threshold, to tune in scenario
# 2.2. to_server without "established", because a SYN arrives before the
# handshake has completed.
drop tcp 10.8.0.0/23 any -> 10.0.2.10 443 (msg:"MAILBRIDGE connection flood to proxy"; flow:to_server; flags:S; detection_filter:track by_src, count 60, seconds 10; classtype:attempted-dos; sid:1000004; rev:2;)
